#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106639} :activity/id #uuid "4aeb9a17-64c0-5bc4-aa52-e694d7a3e9f1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178 :topic/id #uuid "340f0f9d-19b0-5cff-abb5-75d2e7e75aa9" :topic/math-academy-id 1374} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107082 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n![](images/1a/1ac826685473beeb7a7c0ee90465db019d0d5b70b1af562aa3469ae4f0a54007.svg)\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/17/176e4c49ef19bb92d63190dc6674b66ee55b2fdb306293bae65b7f2b2935701d.svg)\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n![](images/a9/a9dd77f0f2ac0f7bd04b5e70fdca3b126d4eb34b0c929caca48c60f138bc644d.svg)\n\nThe **basic variables** are the variables of our system whose coefficients are the pivots of the row echelon form matrix. Let's illustrate the pivots of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1b/1b776faa6c69553ce04fbddc4e1aa45e18d7390a32ce34d9e0317b2fa83b8752.svg)\n\nThe pivots are\n\n- $m_{11}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $x,$ and\n\n- $m_{22}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $y.$\n\nTherefore, the basic variables are $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe remaining variables are called the **free variables**. We have only one free variable in our system, which is $z$.\n\nFinally, we can describe the **general solution** by finding equations for the basic variables in terms of the free variables. In our case, this means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z$.\n\nTo do this, we apply the back substitution. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\ny - 3z = -5\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = -5+3z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-5+3z)+z=2\\,⟹\\, & x=-3+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n![](images/e2/e21bb2b83534152d6bf3d1c43efbd301b42717b975f1f9ebaa28ee4bc3ef31bd.svg)\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n![](images/de/de7a613bbbde25e555608ebc419106701d47875e238832eab7533bf87b0f1ce8.svg)\n\nGeometrically, this is the vector equation of a line, and any point on the line is a solution to our original system.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $z$ and find the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the line. For example, if we choose $z=0,$ then the corresponding values are $x=-3$ and $y=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-3,$ $y=-5,$ $z=0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f5fe61d-1298-5fad-8678-6cd961832dde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3159 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f1588ba-e038-5ac2-b2f0-cd6b05552c88" :step/math-academy-id 17206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105604 :question/id #uuid "ff82d566-9671-5cd2-9c24-f1ac6047ed64" :question/math-academy-id "e-4707"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d865bfb4-6ff5-577a-a992-c481af8842b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058}] :knowledge-poin